#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:1
msgid "Variable Names Plan"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:3
msgid ""
"This plan is *provisional*. It is not agreed upon. It is written with the "
"intention of capturing the desires and concerns of the LLVM community, and "
"forming them into a plan that can be agreed upon. The original author is "
"somewhat naïve in the ways of LLVM so there will inevitably be some details "
"that are flawed. You can help - you can edit this page by submitting a "
"GitHub pull request or reply to the [Request For Comments thread](http://"
"lists.llvm.org/pipermail/llvm-dev/2019-February/130083.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:11
msgid "Too Long; Didn't Read"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:13
msgid "Improve the readability of LLVM code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:15
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:17
msgid ""
"The current [variable naming rule](../CodingStandards.md#name-types-"
"functions-variables-and-enumerators-properly) states:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:20
msgid ""
"Variable names should be nouns (as they represent state). The name should be "
"camel case, and start with an upper case letter (e.g. Leader or Boats)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:23
msgid ""
"This rule is the same as that for type names. This is a problem because the "
"type name cannot be reused for a variable name [^footnote-1]. LLVM "
"developers tend to work around this by either prepending `The` to the type "
"name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:31
msgid ""
"... or more commonly use an acronym, despite the coding standard stating "
"\"Avoid abbreviations unless they are well known\":"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:38
msgid ""
"The proliferation of acronyms leads to hard-to-read code such as [this]"
"(https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/"
"0a8bc14ad7f3209fe702d18e250194cd90188596/llvm/lib/Transforms/Vectorize/"
"LoopVectorize.cpp#L7445):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:45
msgid ""
"Many other coding guidelines [^cite_lldb] [^cite_google] [^cite_webkit] "
"[^cite_qt] [^cite_rust] [^cite_swift] [^cite_python] require that variable "
"names begin with a lower case letter in contrast to class names which begin "
"with a capital letter. This convention means that the most readable variable "
"name also requires the least thought:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:54
msgid ""
"There is some agreement that the current rule is broken [^cite_lattneragree] "
"[^cite_arsenaultagree] [^cite_robinsonagree] and that acronyms are an "
"obstacle to reading new code [^cite_malyutindistinguish] "
"[^cite_carruthacronym] [^cite_picusacronym]. There are some opposing views "
"[^cite_parzyszekacronym2] [^cite_ricciacronyms]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:59
msgid ""
"This work-in-progress proposal is to change the coding standard for variable "
"names to require that they start with a lower case letter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:66
msgid "Variable Names Coding Standard Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:68
msgid ""
"There are two main options for variable names that begin with a lower case "
"letter: `camelBack` and `lower_case`. (These are also known by other names "
"but here we use the terminology from clang-tidy)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:72
msgid ""
"`camelBack` is consistent with [^cite_webkit], [^cite_qt] and [^cite_swift] "
"while `lower_case` is consistent with [^cite_lldb], [^cite_google], "
"[^cite_rust] and [^cite_python]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:75
msgid ""
"`camelBack` is already used for function names, which may be considered an "
"advantage [^cite_lattnerfunction] or a disadvantage [^cite_carruthfunction]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:78
msgid ""
"Approval for `camelBack` was expressed by [^cite_denisovcamelback] "
"[^cite_lattnerfunction] [^cite_ivanovicdistinguish]. Opposition to "
"`camelBack` was expressed by [^cite_carruthcamelback] "
"[^cite_turnercamelback]. Approval for `lower_case` was expressed by "
"[^cite_carruthlower] [^cite_carruthcamelback] [^cite_turnerlldb]. Opposition "
"to `lower_case` was expressed by [^cite_lattnerlower]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:86
msgid "Differentiating variable kinds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:88
msgid ""
"An additional requested change is to distinguish between different kinds of "
"variables [^cite_robinsondistinguish] [^cite_robinsondistinguish2] "
"[^cite_jonesdistinguish] [^cite_ivanovicdistinguish] "
"[^cite_carruthdistinguish] [^cite_malyutindistinguish]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:92
msgid ""
"Others oppose this idea [^cite_hahnledistinguish] [^cite_greenedistinguish] "
"[^cite_hendersonprefix]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:95
msgid ""
"A possibility is for member variables to be prefixed with `m_` and for "
"global variables to be prefixed with `g_` to distinguish them from local "
"variables. This is consistent with [^cite_lldb]. The `m_` prefix is "
"consistent with [^cite_webkit]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:99
msgid ""
"A variation is for member variables to be prefixed with `m` "
"[^cite_ivanovicdistinguish] [^cite_beylsdistinguish]. This is consistent "
"with [^cite_mozilla]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:102
msgid ""
"Another option is for member variables to be suffixed with `_` which is "
"consistent with [^cite_google] and similar to [^cite_python]. Opposed by "
"[^cite_parzyszekdistinguish]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:106
msgid "Reducing the number of acronyms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:108
msgid ""
"While switching coding standard will make it easier to use non-acronym names "
"for new code, it doesn't improve the existing large body of code that uses "
"acronyms extensively to the detriment of its readability. Further, it is "
"natural and generally encouraged that new code be written in the style of "
"the surrounding code. Therefore it is likely that much newly written code "
"will also use acronyms despite what the coding standard says, much as it is "
"today."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:115
msgid ""
"As well as changing the case of variable names, they could also be expanded "
"to their non-acronym form e.g. `Triple T` → `Triple triple`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:118
msgid ""
"There is support for expanding many acronyms [^cite_carruthacronym] "
"[^cite_picusacronym] but there is a preference that expanding acronyms be "
"deferred [^cite_parzyszekacronym] [^cite_carruthacronym]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:122
msgid ""
"The consensus within the community seems to be that at least some acronyms "
"are valuable [^cite_parzyszekacronym] [^cite_lattneracronym]. The most "
"commonly cited acronym is `TLI` however that is used to refer to both "
"`TargetLowering` and `TargetLibraryInfo` [^cite_greenedistinguish]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Proposals/VariableNames.md:127
msgid ""
"The following is a list of acronyms considered sufficiently useful that the "
"benefit of using them outweighs the cost of learning them. Acronyms that are "
"either not on the list or are used to refer to a different type should be "
"expanded."
msgstr ""

